1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,010
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:02,010 --> 00:00:05,990
This is Ria's Slow Podcast.

3
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:11,340
Everyone, now it's May.

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:17,180
May is very special in Korea.

5
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:18,700
It's special.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,900
It's a special month.

7
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,940
A special month.

8
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:27,400
People spend a lot of money in May.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,580
They spend a lot of money.

10
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:32,740
Why do they spend so much money?

11
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,240
Calendar.

12
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,200
Here is a calendar.

13
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,990
This is a Korean May calendar.

14
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:45,880
Look at May 5th on this calendar.

15
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:47,740
May 5th

16
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:52,220
What color is this day marked with?

17
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:55,200
What color is it marked with?

18
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,010
Red.

19
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:58,980
It's red.

20
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,650
It's marked in red.

21
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:07,420
When a date is marked in red like this, it's a day off.

22
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:08,830
A day off.

23
00:01:08,830 --> 00:01:10,850
It's a day off.

24
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:12,430
Day off.

25
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:19,280
No school, no work, everyone rests.

26
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:24,260
This is called a public holiday in Korean.

27
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:25,860
Public holiday.

28
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:33,920
But many people just call it a "red day."

29
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:35,580
Red day.

30
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,530
Because it's red, it's called a red day.

31
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:45,980
But why do people spend a lot of money in May?

32
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:52,600
Looking at the calendar, what day is May 5th?

33
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,750
May 5th is Children's Day.

34
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:58,480
Children.

35
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,780
Children's Day.

36
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:03,870
It's a day made for children.

37
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:06,810
A day made for children.

38
00:02:06,810 --> 00:02:13,280
On this day, parents give gifts to their children.

39
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,200
They give gifts.

40
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:23,760
Or they give gifts to their young nephews and nieces.

41
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:26,460
They always ask,

42
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:28,480
What do you want?

43
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:31,810
What do you want for Children's Day gifts?

44
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:34,460
They ask like this.

45
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:40,680
In Korean supermarkets, there is a big toy section.

46
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:43,040
Toy section.

47
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:48,050
People buy gifts here.

48
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:52,660
They buy gifts to give to children.

49
00:02:52,660 --> 00:03:00,050
Earlier, I said that on red days, public holidays, people rest.

50
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:07,360
So on this day, wherever you go in Korea, there are many people.

51
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,530
Wherever you go, there are many people.

52
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:14,540
Wherever you go, there are too many people.

53
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:20,230
Especially, parents go to amusement parks a lot.

54
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:26,350
Parents go to amusement parks with their children a lot.

55
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:31,940
Do you have Children's Day in your country?

56
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:35,780
Next is May 8th.

57
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:37,630
May 8th.

58
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:40,900
It's Parents' Day.

59
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:42,560
Parents' Day.

60
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:50,380
Parents' Day combines Mother's Day and Father's Day.

61
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:52,230
It combines them.

62
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:55,010
To combine.

63
00:03:55,010 --> 00:04:04,990
Um... As far as I know, in foreign countries, Mother's Day and Father's Day are separate.

64
00:04:04,990 --> 00:04:06,640
They are separate.

65
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:08,030
Right?

66
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:11,740
In Korea, there is Parents' Day.

67
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:16,130
Wow, this is when people spend the most money.

68
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:19,220
They spend the most money.

69
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:29,270
Since Mother's Day and Father's Day are combined, do you have to buy a few gifts?

70
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:32,670
How many gifts do you have to prepare?

71
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:35,390
Two gifts!

72
00:04:35,390 --> 00:04:39,620
You have to prepare two gifts.

73
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:45,330
And you buy red carnations.

74
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:47,400
Carnation flowers.

75
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:55,530
The flower language of carnations means 'love, respect, gratitude.'

76
00:04:55,530 --> 00:05:05,280
But there are words for mom and dad, and also words for mother and father.

77
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,060
What is the difference?

78
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:16,690
Mother is a more respectful word for mom, and father is a more respectful word for dad.

79
00:05:16,690 --> 00:05:22,720
Then when do you use the words 'mother' and 'father'?

80
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:32,210
Actually, young people don't use the words mother and father much in daily life.

81
00:05:32,210 --> 00:05:37,620
They just call them mom and dad.

82
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:48,860
I have never seriously called my parents 'mother' or 'father' even once in my life.

83
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:55,170
I have never seriously called them 'mother' or 'father' even once.

84
00:05:55,170 --> 00:06:01,240
But sometimes I call my dad 'father' jokingly.

85
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:03,870
When is that?

86
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:09,300
When I was young, I received pocket money.

87
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:10,540
Pocket money.

88
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:20,000
At that time, I said, "Thank you, father!" kind of jokingly.

89
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:28,190
Also, there is a cuter way to say father in Korean, 'Abuji.'

90
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:29,550
Abuji.

91
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:34,420
Sometimes I call him Abuji.

92
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:40,430
This is an actual text message I sent to my dad.

93
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:43,030
"Thank you, Abuji."

94
00:06:43,030 --> 00:06:45,550
I wrote like this.

95
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:54,200
Anyway, for Koreans, Parents' Day is really, really important.

96
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:58,510
In Korea, there is a word called 'hyo-do.'

97
00:06:58,510 --> 00:06:59,670
Hyo-do (filial piety)

98
00:06:59,670 --> 00:07:08,620
To explain simply, it means respecting and being grateful to your parents.

99
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:15,260
Taking good care of your parents with a thankful heart.

100
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:16,670
To take care.

101
00:07:16,670 --> 00:07:19,070
Taking good care.

102
00:07:19,070 --> 00:07:23,680
So it's a very meaningful day.

103
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,690
Meaningful day.

104
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:32,740
But sometimes I feel a bit burdened on this day.

105
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:34,600
I feel burdened.

106
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:38,340
I sometimes feel a bit burdened.

107
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:41,020
I sometimes feel burdened.

108
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:48,440
Some Korean parents really like to compare.

109
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:49,860
Comparing.

110
00:07:49,860 --> 00:07:58,370
For example, "Ria, I heard other kids got more expensive gifts."

111
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:01,900
"I heard they got more expensive gifts."

112
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:04,470
This is comparison.

113
00:08:04,470 --> 00:08:07,540
Comparisons parents make.

114
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:11,370
Comparing is not good, right?

115
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:17,660
Actually, my parents don't put pressure on me.

116
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,230
"Give expensive gifts."

117
00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:22,680
They don't pressure me like that.

118
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:28,350
But I just feel that way myself.

119
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:33,630
Yes, next is May 15th.

120
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:35,550
May 15th.

121
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:40,890
It says here it's Teacher's Day.

122
00:08:40,890 --> 00:08:42,660
Teacher's Day.

123
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:44,370
Teacher.

124
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:46,440
What is a teacher?

125
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:47,390
Teacher.

126
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:52,540
Ah, the answer is already here.

127
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:53,540
Teacher's Day.

128
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:56,690
It's the day of teachers.

129
00:08:56,690 --> 00:08:58,250
Teacher.

130
00:08:58,250 --> 00:09:02,690
'Seuseung' is a respectful word for teacher.

131
00:09:02,690 --> 00:09:09,320
When you say "Seuseung-nim!" it feels very respectful.

132
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:14,920
I have never used this word even once.

133
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:26,590
It's a feeling of a teacher who teaches not only English or Korean but also teaches you about life.

134
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:29,020
That's the feeling.

135
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:35,260
I used to teach young students a long time ago.

136
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:44,930
Every year on Teacher's Day, those students gave me gifts.

137
00:09:44,930 --> 00:09:47,210
They also gave me letters.

138
00:09:47,210 --> 00:09:48,060
Letters!

139
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:54,780
I remember being very touched when I first received them.

140
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:58,180
I remember being very touched.

141
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:03,710
There was a student who was usually very cold.

142
00:10:03,710 --> 00:10:05,960
Cold student.

143
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:07,940
Cold.

144
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:10,940
A bit cold student.

145
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:16,640
That student suddenly gave me a letter on Teacher's Day.

146
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:20,020
So I was surprised.

147
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:21,540
I was surprised.

148
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:26,300
I really like letters.

149
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:33,500
With letters, you can say things you usually can't say.

150
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:38,220
You can say things you are too shy to say.

151
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:40,860
You can write them down.

152
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:43,220
You can write them down.

153
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:48,420
I really miss those students.

154
00:10:48,420 --> 00:10:51,260
I miss them a lot.

155
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:55,700
Of course, I don't mean I miss the gifts.

156
00:10:55,700 --> 00:11:05,180
I miss the students' hearts, their pure hearts, their laughter.

157
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:08,060
Please don't misunderstand.

158
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:09,820
Don't misunderstand!

159
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:17,180
The next big public holiday is Buddha's Birthday.

160
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:19,300
Buddha's Birthday.

161
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:25,250
This day commemorates the birth of Buddha.

162
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:26,890
To commemorate.

163
00:11:26,890 --> 00:11:34,330
On this day, people go to temples to pray or hang lanterns.

164
00:11:34,330 --> 00:11:35,390
Hang.

165
00:11:35,390 --> 00:11:44,930
If you come to Korea in May, be sure to visit a temple.

166
00:11:44,930 --> 00:11:46,260
Temple!

167
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:49,940
There is a big temple in Seoul.

168
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:52,730
It's called 'Bongeunsa.'

169
00:11:52,730 --> 00:11:54,300
Bongeunsa.

170
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:58,160
Many foreigners also visit this place.

171
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:02,540
You don't have to be Buddhist.

172
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:06,460
You can go even if you're not Buddhist.

173
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:09,760
Anyone can go.

174
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:17,490
If you go at this time, there will be lots of very pretty colorful lanterns.

175
00:12:17,490 --> 00:12:22,620
There will be lots of very pretty colorful lanterns.

176
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:33,120
So today, I talked about Korea's May public holidays, the red days.

177
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:39,910
I said May is the month when people spend the most money.

178
00:12:39,910 --> 00:12:47,970
Actually, even though people spend a lot of money, it's the warmest month in terms of heart.

179
00:12:47,970 --> 00:12:55,420
Because people take care of the children at home and also their parents.

180
